known_relatives.claimed_difference:
Parent B moves V particles in R^d by Euler substeps, at O(S V d) per token. It keeps class membership only up to a snowplow gap.

Here:
- co-location is exact;
- the image sheet is applied at read time to stamped tokens whose code-sheet image contains the residual units, and no per-token position is ever updated;
- a write costs O(K_max M);
- class transfer composes with code-sheet rules through packing.

Those systems fire one winning rule over hand-written conditions. Here:
- every disjoint match fires, which gives composition;
- each condition is the residual against the memory's own two-stage prediction;
- a second rule layer rewrites the first layer's outputs, but only for tokens demonstrated before that rule was written.

This last point is the class mechanism, and a flat rule base has no counterpart to it.

state.meaning:
R holds K_max rows plus one integer K (0 <= K <= K_max), the number of rows in use. K_max is the pool size; K_max = 8 in all claims and in the toy.

Rows 1..K are numbered from 1 in recruitment order. Row j holds three things:
- a sheet flag l_j in {0,1} (0 = code sheet, 1 = image sheet);
- an address a_j in {0,1}^M;
- an addition pattern b_j in {0,1}^M.

The specificity of row j is |a_j| = sum of a_j. A row is never modified after it is written. K = 0 at the start of each context.

These temporaries are the visiting order and the fire flags s_j of the packing operator P, plus eight length-M integer vectors:
- o1: the code-sheet image;
- U1: the units claimed on the code sheet;
- o: the final prediction;
- U2: the units claimed on the image sheet;
- r = E_y - o: the residual;
- D = [r<0]: the units the prediction has but the target lacks;
- A = [r>0]: the units the target has but the prediction lacks;
- beta: the base code of the write.

Here [.] is the elementwise Iverson bracket.
